"The 2024–2026 CFA Program curriculum reflects the largest structural modernization in CFA Institute history—transitioning from long-form academic chapters to focused Learning Modules (LMs) at Level I (while shifting foundational accounting, mechanics, and time-value-of-money material into Prerequisite Readings and Practical Skills Modules [PSMs]) and maintaining 45 vignette-driven Learning Modules at Level II. Between 2024, 2025, and 2026, the core syllabus across Financial Statement Analysis, Fixed Income, Equity Investments, and Portfolio Management has remained highly stable, with third-party prep providers (Kaplan SchweserNotes and Wiley Efficient Learning / UWorld Finance) aligning their module numbering directly 1-to-1 with CFA Institute's official Learning Modules.\n\nHigh-Level Curriculum Architecture & Exam Weight Matrix (2024–2026)\nCore Subject Area\tLevel I Weight\tLevel I Official LMs\tLevel II Weight\tLevel II Official LMs\tKaplan Schweser Volume Mapping\tWiley / UWorld Study Guide Mapping\n1. Financial Statement Analysis (FSA)\t11%–14%\t12 LMs (+7 Prereq Modules)\t10%–15%\t6 LMs\tL1: Book 2 / L2: Book 2\tL1: Vol. 2 (FSA) / L2: Vol. 2 (FSA)\n2. Fixed Income (FI)\t11%–14%\t19 LMs\t10%–15%\t5 LMs\tL1: Book 4 / L2: Book 4\tL1: Vol. 4 (FI) / L2: Vol. 4 (FI)\n3. Equity Investments / Valuation (EQ)\t11%–14%\t8 LMs\t10%–15%\t6 LMs\tL1: Book 3 / L2: Book 3\tL1: Vol. 3 (Equity) / L2: Vol. 3 (Equity)\n4. Portfolio Management (PM)\t8%–12%\t6 LMs\t10%–15%\t6 LMs (5 in 2026)*\tL1: Book 5 / L2: Book 5\tL1: Vol. 5 (PM) / L2: Vol. 5 (PM)\n\n*Note: In the 2025–2026 Level II cycle, \"Trading Cost and Electronic Markets\" was retired from Level II Portfolio Management, leaving 5–6 active modules depending on the cycle year.\n\n1. Financial Statement Analysis (FSA)\n1.1 Complete Learning Module & Prep Provider Cross-Reference\nLevel I Financial Statement Analysis (12 Learning Modules + Prerequisite Mechanics)\n\n(Note: In 2024–2026, CFA Institute moved introductory double-entry bookkeeping, accrual mechanics, and basic financial statement definitions to the FSA Prerequisite Readings, which Kaplan Schweser and UWorld provide as supplemental digital Vol. 0 / Prerequisite Foundations.)\n\nOfficial CFAI LM #\tOfficial CFA Institute Learning Module Title (2024–2026)\tKaplan SchweserNotes Equivalent\tWiley / UWorld Equivalent & Structural Notes\nLM 1\tIntroduction to Financial Statement Analysis\tBook 2, Module 13 (LM 1)\tL1 FSA Study Guide – Module 1: Audit Reports, Regulatory Filings & 6-Step Framework\nLM 2\tAnalyzing Income Statements\tBook 2, Module 14 (LM 2)\tL1 FSA Study Guide – Module 2: Revenue Recognition, Expense Grouping, Basic/Diluted EPS\nLM 3\tAnalyzing Balance Sheets\tBook 2, Module 15 (LM 3)\tL1 FSA Study Guide – Module 3: Intangibles, Goodwill, Financial Instruments (IFRS 9), Equity\nLM 4\tAnalyzing Statements of Cash Flows I\tBook 2, Module 16 (LM 4)\tL1 FSA Study Guide – Module 4: CFO/CFI/CFF Classification, Direct vs. Indirect CFO\nLM 5\tAnalyzing Statements of Cash Flows II\tBook 2, Module 17 (LM 5)\tL1 FSA Study Guide – Module 5: Common-Size Cash Flows, FCFF & FCFE Derivations\nLM 6\tAnalysis of Inventories\tBook 2, Module 18 (LM 6)\tL1 FSA Study Guide – Module 6: FIFO vs. LIFO, LIFO Reserve/Liquidation, NRV vs. LCNRV\nLM 7\tAnalysis of Long-Term Assets\tBook 2, Module 19 (LM 7)\tL1 FSA Study Guide – Module 7: Capitalization vs. Expensing, Revaluation, Impairment, Derecognition\nLM 8\tTopics in Long-Term Liabilities and Equity\tBook 2, Module 20 (LM 8)\tL1 FSA Study Guide – Module 8: Lessee/Lessor Accounting, Defined Benefit vs. Contribution, Share-Based Comp\nLM 9\tAnalysis of Income Taxes\tBook 2, Module 21 (LM 9)\tL1 FSA Study Guide – Module 9: DTA/DTL, Tax Base vs. Carrying Value, Valuation Allowance, Effective Tax Rate\nLM 10\tFinancial Reporting Quality\tBook 2, Module 22 (LM 10)\tL1 FSA Study Guide – Module 10: Earnings Quality Spectrum, Beneish M-Score Concepts, Accounting Warning Signs\nLM 11\tFinancial Analysis Techniques\tBook 2, Module 23 (LM 11)\tL1 FSA Study Guide – Module 11: Activity, Liquidity, Solvency, Profitability & 3/5-Way DuPont Decomposition\nLM 12\tIntroduction to Financial Statement Modeling\tBook 2, Module 24 (LM 12)\tL1 FSA Study Guide – Module 12: Pro Forma Income/Balance Sheet/Cash Flow Forecasting & Behavioral Biases\nLevel II Financial Statement Analysis (6 Learning Modules)\nOfficial CFAI LM #\tOfficial CFA Institute Learning Module Title (2024–2026)\tKaplan SchweserNotes Equivalent\tWiley / UWorld Equivalent & Structural Notes\nLM 1\tIntercorporate Investments\tBook 2, Reading 10 (LM 1)\tL2 FSA Study Guide – Module 1: Financial Assets, Associates (Equity Method), Joint Ventures, Acquisitions, VIEs/SPEs\nLM 2\tEmployee Compensation: Post-Employment and Share-Based\tBook 2, Reading 11 (LM 2)\tL2 FSA Study Guide – Module 2: DB Pension Accounting (P&L vs. OCI), Share-Based Comp (RSUs, Stock Options, Treasury Stock Method)\nLM 3\tMultinational Operations\tBook 2, Reading 12 (LM 3)\tL2 FSA Study Guide – Module 3: Functional Currency, Current Rate vs. Temporal Method, Hyperinflation, Effective Tax Rate\nLM 4\tAnalysis of Financial Institutions\tBook 2, Reading 13 (LM 4)\tL2 FSA Study Guide – Module 4: CAMELS Framework, Basel III Capital/Liquidity Ratios (CET1, LCR, NSFR), P&C vs. Life Insurers\nLM 5\tEvaluating Quality of Financial Reports\tBook 2, Reading 14 (LM 5)\tL2 FSA Study Guide – Module 5: Beneish M-Score, Accruals Ratio (Balance Sheet vs. Cash Flow), Cash Flow Persistence\nLM 6\tIntegration of Financial Statement Analysis Techniques\tBook 2, Reading 15 (LM 6)\tL2 FSA Study Guide – Module 6: Off-Balance-Sheet Adjustments, NOA, DuPont, Segment & Balance Sheet Triage\n1.2 Key Subtopics, Core Formulas & Critical IFRS vs. US GAAP Differences\nCore FSA Formulas (Level I & Level II)\n\nBasic & Diluted Earnings Per Share (EPS):\n\nBasic EPS\n=\nNet Income\n−\nPreferred Dividends\nWeighted Average Common Shares Outstanding (WACSO)\nBasic EPS=\nWeighted Average Common Shares Outstanding (WACSO)\nNet Income−Preferred Dividends\n\t​\n\nDiluted EPS\n=\nNet Income\n−\nPreferred Dividends\n+\nConvertible Pref. Div.\n+\nAfter-Tax Convertible Debt Interest\nWACSO\n+\nNew Shares from Conv. Pref.\n+\nNew Shares from Conv. Debt\n+\nNet Option/Warrant Shares (Treasury Stock Method)\nDiluted EPS=\nWACSO+New Shares from Conv. Pref.+New Shares from Conv. Debt+Net Option/Warrant Shares (Treasury Stock Method)\nNet Income−Preferred Dividends+Convertible Pref. Div.+After-Tax Convertible Debt Interest\n\t​\n\n\nTreasury Stock Method (Options/Warrants):\n\nNet New Shares\n=\n𝑁\noptions\n−\n(\n𝑁\noptions\n×\nExercise Price\nAverage Market Price\n)\nNet New Shares=N\noptions\n\t​\n\n−(\nAverage Market Price\nN\noptions\n\t​\n\n×Exercise Price\n\t​\n\n)\n\n(Note: At Level II LM 2, unamortized share-based compensation expense is added to the assumed proceeds from exercise when computing treasury stock buybacks).\n\nFree Cash Flow to the Firm (FCFF) & Free Cash Flow to Equity (FCFE) from Statement of Cash Flows:\n\nFCFF\n=\nNI\n+\nNCC\n+\nInt\n(\n1\n−\n𝑡\n)\n−\nFCInv\n−\nWCInv\n=\nCFO\n+\nInt\n(\n1\n−\n𝑡\n)\n−\nFCInv\nFCFF=NI+NCC+Int(1−t)−FCInv−WCInv=CFO+Int(1−t)−FCInv\nFCFE\n=\nCFO\n−\nFCInv\n+\nNet Borrowing\nFCFE=CFO−FCInv+Net Borrowing\n\n(Under IFRS, if interest paid was classified in Financing [CFF], do not add back \nInt\n(\n1\n−\n𝑡\n)\nInt(1−t) to CFO when calculating FCFF; if interest paid was deducted in CFO, deduct nothing further for FCFE).\n\nFIFO vs. LIFO Inventory Conversions (US GAAP):\n\nInventory\nFIFO\n=\nInventory\nLIFO\n+\nLIFO Reserve\nInventory\nFIFO\n\t​\n\n=Inventory\nLIFO\n\t​\n\n+LIFO Reserve\nCOGS\nFIFO\n=\nCOGS\nLIFO\n−\nΔ\nLIFO Reserve\nCOGS\nFIFO\n\t​\n\n=COGS\nLIFO\n\t​\n\n−ΔLIFO Reserve\nRetained Earnings\nFIFO\n=\nRetained Earnings\nLIFO\n+\nLIFO Reserve\n×\n(\n1\n−\n𝑡\n)\nRetained Earnings\nFIFO\n\t​\n\n=Retained Earnings\nLIFO\n\t​\n\n+LIFO Reserve×(1−t)\n\n5-Way DuPont Decomposition of ROE:\n\nROE\n=\n(\nNI\nEBT\n)\n×\n(\nEBT\nEBIT\n)\n×\n(\nEBIT\nRevenue\n)\n×\n(\nRevenue\nAvg. Total Assets\n)\n×\n(\nAvg. Total Assets\nAvg. Shareholders’ Equity\n)\nROE=(\nEBT\nNI\n\t​\n\n)×(\nEBIT\nEBT\n\t​\n\n)×(\nRevenue\nEBIT\n\t​\n\n)×(\nAvg. Total Assets\nRevenue\n\t​\n\n)×(\nAvg. Shareholders’ Equity\nAvg. Total Assets\n\t​\n\n)\nROE\n=\nTax Burden\n×\nInterest Burden\n×\nEBIT Margin\n×\nTotal Asset Turnover\n×\nFinancial Leverage\nROE=Tax Burden×Interest Burden×EBIT Margin×Total Asset Turnover×Financial Leverage\n\nDeferred Tax Liabilities & Assets (DTL / DTA):\n\nIncome Tax Expense\n=\nTaxes Payable\n+\nΔ\nDTL\n−\nΔ\nDTA\nIncome Tax Expense=Taxes Payable+ΔDTL−ΔDTA\n\nLevel II Defined Benefit Pension Accounting:\n\nFunded Status\n=\nFair Value of Plan Assets\n−\nProjected Benefit Obligation (PBO)\nFunded Status=Fair Value of Plan Assets−Projected Benefit Obligation (PBO)\nTotal Periodic Pension Cost (TPPC)\n=\nCurrent Service Cost\n+\nPast Service Cost\n+\nInterest Cost\n−\nActual Return on Plan Assets\n±\nActuarial Losses/(Gains)\nTotal Periodic Pension Cost (TPPC)=Current Service Cost+Past Service Cost+Interest Cost−Actual Return on Plan Assets±Actuarial Losses/(Gains)\nTPPC\n=\nEmployer Contributions\n−\nΔ\nFunded Status\nTPPC=Employer Contributions−ΔFunded Status\n\nLevel II Balance Sheet & Cash Flow Based Accruals Ratios:\n\nAccruals\nBS\n=\nΔ\nNOA\n=\n(\nOperating Assets\n𝑡\n−\nOperating Liabilities\n𝑡\n)\n−\n(\nOperating Assets\n𝑡\n−\n1\n−\nOperating Liabilities\n𝑡\n−\n1\n)\nAccruals\nBS\n\t​\n\n=ΔNOA=(Operating Assets\nt\n\t​\n\n−Operating Liabilities\nt\n\t​\n\n)−(Operating Assets\nt−1\n\t​\n\n−Operating Liabilities\nt−1\n\t​\n\n)\nAccruals Ratio\nBS\n=\nNOA\n𝑡\n−\nNOA\n𝑡\n−\n1\n1\n2\n(\nNOA\n𝑡\n+\nNOA\n𝑡\n−\n1\n)\n,\nAccruals Ratio\nCF\n=\nNI\n−\n(\nCFO\n+\nCFI\n)\n1\n2\n(\nNOA\n𝑡\n+\nNOA\n𝑡\n−\n1\n)\nAccruals Ratio\nBS\n\t​\n\n=\n2\n1\n\t​\n\n(NOA\nt\n\t​\n\n+NOA\nt−1\n\t​\n\n)\nNOA\nt\n\t​\n\n−NOA\nt−1\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n,Accruals Ratio\nCF\n\t​\n\n=\n2\n1\n\t​\n\n(NOA\nt\n\t​\n\n+NOA\nt−1\n\t​\n\n)\nNI−(CFO+CFI)\n\t​\n\nMaster IFRS vs. US GAAP Reconciliation Table (Level I & Level II)\nAccounting Area\tIFRS Treatment (IAS / IFRS)\tUS GAAP Treatment (ASC)\tRatio & Valuation Impact\nCash Flow Classification (LM 4)\tInterest Paid: CFO or CFF. Interest/Dividends Received: CFO or CFI. Dividends Paid: CFO or CFF.\tInterest Paid/Received & Dividends Received: Strictly CFO. Dividends Paid: Strictly CFF.\tAffects reported CFO directly; analysts must adjust CFO before computing FCFF/FCFE under IFRS if interest/dividends are misclassified.\nInventory Costing & Write-Downs (LM 6)\tLIFO prohibited. Valued at Lower of Cost or Net Realizable Value (NRV). Reversal of write-downs permitted (up to original write-down).\tLIFO permitted (subject to LIFO conformity rule). Valued at LCNRV (FIFO/weighted avg) or Lower of Cost or Market (LCM for LIFO/retail). Reversals prohibited.\tIn inflationary periods, LIFO gives lower ending inventory, higher COGS, lower net income, and higher CFO (due to lower cash taxes).\nLong-Lived Asset Valuation & Impairment (LM 7)\tCost Model or Revaluation Model allowed. 1-step impairment test: Carrying Amt vs. Recoverable Amt (\nmax[Fair Value less Cost to Sell,Value in Use]). Impairment reversal permitted (except goodwill).\tCost Model only (revaluation prohibited). 2-step impairment test: (1) Recoverability test (Carrying Amt vs. undiscounted future CFs); (2) Loss = Carrying Amt minus Fair Value. Reversal prohibited.\tUpward revaluation increases equity (Revaluation Surplus in OCI) and future depreciation, depressing future ROA and ROE.\nIntangible R&D Costs (LM 7)\tResearch: Expensed. Development: Capitalized once technological/commercial feasibility criteria are met.\tBoth Research & Development: Expensed immediately (except software development after technological feasibility is reached & motion pictures).\tIFRS capitalization increases near-term Net Income, Assets, and CFO (outflows go to CFI), while lowering near-term CFI.\nLessee Accounting (LM 8)\tSingle model: All leases (except short-term/low-value) are Finance Leases on balance sheet. Amortization of ROU asset and interest expense recognized separately.\tDual model: Both Finance and Operating leases put a Right-of-Use (ROU) asset and Lease Liability on balance sheet, but Operating Leases record a single straight-line lease expense on I/S and keep entire payment in CFO.\tUnder IFRS, EBITDA and CFO are higher for operating-style leases because lease payments are split into depreciation and interest (often CFF) rather than a single operating lease expense.\nIntercorporate Investments: Financial Assets (L2 LM 1)\tIFRS 9: Amortized Cost (debt only, SPPI + held-to-collect), FVOCI (debt or irrevocable election for equity—no recycling of equity gains to P&L), or FVPL.\tASC 321/320: Held-to-Maturity (debt at amortized cost), Available-for-Sale (debt only at FVOCI with recycling), Trading / All Equity Securities at FVPL.\tUnder IFRS, equity investments designated at FVOCI never flow unrealized gains/losses through Net Income, lowering earnings volatility compared to US GAAP.\nBusiness Combinations & Goodwill (L2 LM 1)\tFull Goodwill or Partial Goodwill permitted. Non-controlling interest (NCI) measured at either full fair value or proportionate share of acquiree's identifiable net assets.\tFull Goodwill mandatory. NCI must be measured at full fair value on acquisition date.\tFull goodwill inflates both Total Assets (Goodwill) and Total Equity (NCI) relative to partial goodwill, lowering ROA and ROE.\nDefined Benefit Pensions (L2 LM 2)\tP&L: Current + Past Service Cost + Net Interest Income/Expense (Funded Status \n× Discount Rate). OCI: Actuarial gains/losses and difference between actual return and interest income on plan assets (never recycled/amortized to P&L).\tP&L: Current Service Cost + Interest Cost on PBO \n− Expected Return on Plan Assets + Amortization of Past Service Cost + Corridor Amortization of Actuarial Gains/Losses. OCI: Unamortized PSC and actuarial items.\tUS GAAP generally reports higher operating income when Expected Return on Plan Assets exceeds the discount rate, and smooths volatility via corridor amortization.\nHyperinflationary Subsidiaries (L2 LM 3)\tIAS 29: First restate non-monetary assets/liabilities and all I/S items for local general price-level inflation (purchasing power gain/loss in P&L), then translate all items at the current exchange rate.\tASC 830: Functional currency is forced to be the parent's presentation currency; remeasure foreign financial statements using the Temporal Method (remeasurement gain/loss in P&L).\tVastly different balance sheet carrying values for PPE/Inventory and different translation/purchasing-power P&L impacts.\n1.3 Graded Numerical Calculation Audit (Easy to Difficult)\n\nFundamental / Easy:\n\nComputing WACSO with stock dividends and stock splits applied retroactively to the beginning of the year (or issuance date).\n\nConverting Indirect CFO to Direct CFO (e.g., \nCash Collected from Customers\n=\nRevenue\n−\nΔ\nAccounts Receivable\n+\nΔ\nUnearned Revenue\nCash Collected from Customers=Revenue−ΔAccounts Receivable+ΔUnearned Revenue).\n\nStraight-line vs. Double-Declining Balance (DDB) depreciation and gain/loss on asset disposal.\n\nComputing 3-Way and 5-Way DuPont ROE and Cash Conversion Cycle (\nDOH\n+\nDSO\n−\nDPO\nDOH+DSO−DPO).\n\nIntermediate:\n\nDiluted EPS ranking test: Ordering convertible preferred stock, convertible bonds, and stock options by incremental dilution impact and excluding antidilutive securities sequentially.\n\nLIFO-to-FIFO Financial Statement Conversion: Adjusting COGS, Ending Inventory, Cash, DTL, and Retained Earnings, and recomputing Current Ratio, Inventory Turnover, and Net Profit Margin, plus quantifying the gross profit distortion from a LIFO Liquidation.\n\nLessee Balance Sheet & Income Statement Amortization: Calculating initial ROU Asset and Lease Liability PV, splitting annual lease payment into interest expense and principal reduction, and contrasting IFRS vs. US GAAP ROU asset amortization schedules.\n\nComplex / Difficult (Level II Vignettes):\n\nEquity Method with Upstream/Downstream Inventory Transfers & Excess Purchase Price Amortization:\n\nEquity Income\n=\n%\nown\n×\n(\nNet Income\nsub\n−\nAmortization of Fair Value Excess\n−\nUnrealized Profit in Ending Inventory\n+\nRealized Profit from Prior Period Inventory\n)\nEquity Income=%\nown\n\t​\n\n×(Net Income\nsub\n\t​\n\n−Amortization of Fair Value Excess−Unrealized Profit in Ending Inventory+Realized Profit from Prior Period Inventory)\n\nAcquisition Method Consolidation with Full vs. Partial Goodwill & Impairment Testing: Computing consolidated assets, liabilities, and NCI at acquisition; testing goodwill impairment under IFRS (1-step recoverable amount vs. carrying value of cash-generating unit) vs. US GAAP (carrying value vs. fair value of reporting unit).\n\nDefined Benefit Pension Reclassification (Economic vs. Reported Pension Expense): Reconciling beginning PBO to ending PBO and beginning Plan Assets to ending Plan Assets; calculating reported pension expense under IFRS vs. US GAAP; and adjusting Operating Income, Interest Expense, and Cash Flow from Operations (CFO) when Employer Contributions exceed Total Periodic Pension Cost (reclassifying the excess contribution after tax from CFO to CFF as an economic debt repayment).\n\nMultinational Currency Translation (Current Rate Method vs. Temporal Method): Translating an international subsidiary's balance sheet and income statement under both methods, determining the Cumulative Translation Adjustment (CTA) in OCI (Current Rate) vs. the Remeasurement Gain/Loss in Net Income (Temporal based on net monetary asset or net monetary liability exposure), and evaluating how each method distorts Gross Margin, Inventory Turnover, and Leverage ratios when the foreign currency appreciates or depreciates.\n\n2. Fixed Income (FI)\n2.1 Complete Learning Module & Prep Provider Cross-Reference\nLevel I Fixed Income (19 Learning Modules)\n\n(Note: In 2024–2026, CFA Institute split the former 6 bulky Fixed Income readings into 19 modular LMs. Both Kaplan Schweser and UWorld adopted this exact 19-module sequence.)\n\nOfficial CFAI LM #\tOfficial CFA Institute Learning Module Title (2024–2026)\tKaplan SchweserNotes Equivalent\tWiley / UWorld Equivalent & Structural Notes\nLM 1\tFixed-Income Instrument Features\tBook 4, Module 39 (LM 1)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 1: Indenture, Covenants, Seniority, Contingency Provisions\nLM 2\tFixed-Income Cash Flows and Types\tBook 4, Module 40 (LM 2)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 2: Bullet, Amortizing, Sinking Fund, FRNs, TIPS, Callable/Putable/Convertible\nLM 3\tFixed-Income Issuance and Trading\tBook 4, Module 41 (LM 3)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 3: Primary Auctions, Shelf Registration, OTC Dealer Markets, Bond Indexes\nLM 4\tFixed-Income Markets for Corporate Issuers\tBook 4, Module 42 (LM 4)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 4: Commercial Paper, Repos, Repo Margin/Haircut, IG vs. High-Yield\nLM 5\tFixed-Income Markets for Government Issuers\tBook 4, Module 43 (LM 5)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 5: Sovereign Debt Auctions, Agency/Quasi-Government, Munis (GO vs. Revenue)\nLM 6\tFixed-Income Bond Valuation: Prices and Yields\tBook 4, Module 44 (LM 6)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 6: DCF Pricing, Flat vs. Full Price, Accrued Interest (Actual/Actual vs. 30/360), Matrix Pricing\nLM 7\tYield and Yield Spread Measures for Fixed-Rate Bonds\tBook 4, Module 45 (LM 7)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 7: Periodicity Conversion, Street vs. True Yield, Current Yield, YTC/YTW, G/I/Z-Spreads, OAS\nLM 8\tYield and Yield Spread Measures for Floating-Rate Instruments\tBook 4, Module 46 (LM 8)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 8: FRN Quoted Margin vs. Discount Margin, Money Market Discount Rate vs. Add-On Rate\nLM 9\tThe Term Structure of Interest Rates: Spot, Par, and Forward Curves\tBook 4, Module 47 (LM 9)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 9: Bootstrapping Spot Rates, Par Curve, Implied Forward Rates (\nf\nA,B−A\n\t​\n\n)\nLM 10\tInterest Rate Risk and Return\tBook 4, Module 48 (LM 10)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 10: Horizon Yield, Reinvestment Risk vs. Price Risk, Macaulay Duration, Duration Gap\nLM 11\tYield-Based Bond Duration Measures and Properties\tBook 4, Module 49 (LM 11)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 11: Modified Duration, Approx. ModDur, Money Duration, PVBP\nLM 12\tYield-Based Bond Convexity and Portfolio Properties\tBook 4, Module 50 (LM 12)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 12: Convexity, Taylor Expansion Price Change, Portfolio Duration & Limitations\nLM 13\tCurve-Based and Empirical Fixed-Income Risk Measures\tBook 4, Module 51 (LM 13)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 13: Effective Duration/Convexity, Key Rate Duration, Analytical vs. Empirical Duration\nLM 14\tCredit Risk\tBook 4, Module 52 (LM 14)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 14: POD, LGD, Expected Loss, Credit Ratings, Notching, Return Impact of Spread Changes\nLM 15\tCredit Analysis for Government Issuers\tBook 4, Module 53 (LM 15)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 15: Qualitative & Quantitative Sovereign Credit Factors, Local vs. Foreign Currency Debt\nLM 16\tCredit Analysis for Corporate Issuers\tBook 4, Module 54 (LM 16)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 16: 4 Cs of Credit, Credit Ratios (EBITDA/Interest, Debt/EBITDA, FCF/Debt), Seniority\nLM 17\tFixed-Income Securitization\tBook 4, Module 55 (LM 17)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 17: SPE/SPV Mechanics, Bankruptcy Remoteness, Subordination/Waterfalls, Covered Bonds\nLM 18\tAsset-Backed Security (ABS) Instrument and Market Features\tBook 4, Module 56 (LM 18)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 18: Auto Loan ABS, Credit Card ABS (Lockout Period), CDOs, CLOs\nLM 19\tMortgage-Backed Security (MBS) Instrument and Market Features\tBook 4, Module 57 (LM 19)\tL1 FI Study Guide – Module 19: LTV/DTI, Agency RMBS Pass-Throughs, Prepayment Risk (Contraction/Extension), CMOs (Sequential, PAC/Support), CMBS (DSCR)\nLevel II Fixed Income (5 Learning Modules)\nOfficial CFAI LM #\tOfficial CFA Institute Learning Module Title (2024–2026)\tKaplan SchweserNotes Equivalent\tWiley / UWorld Equivalent & Structural Notes\nLM 1\tThe Term Structure and Interest Rate Dynamics\tBook 4, Reading 22 (LM 1)\tL2 FI Study Guide – Module 1: Spot/Forward Pricing, Swap Curve, Z-Spread, TED/SOFR-OIS, Term Structure Theories, Vasicek/CIR/Ho-Lee, Key Rate Durations\nLM 2\tThe Arbitrage-Free Valuation Framework\tBook 4, Reading 23 (LM 2)\tL2 FI Study Guide – Module 2: Law of One Price, Stripping/Reconstitution, Binomial Interest Rate Trees, Calibration, Pathwise Valuation, Monte Carlo\nLM 3\tValuation and Analysis of Bonds with Embedded Options\tBook 4, Reading 24 (LM 3)\tL2 FI Study Guide – Module 3: Callable/Putable Pricing via Binomial Trees, Volatility Effects, OAS, Effective Duration/Convexity, Capped/Floored FRNs, Convertibles\nLM 4\tCredit Analysis Models\tBook 4, Reading 25 (LM 4)\tL2 FI Study Guide – Module 4: Credit Valuation Adjustment (CVA), Credit Migration, Structural (Merton) vs. Reduced-Form Models, Credit Spread Term Structure\nLM 5\tCredit Default Swaps\tBook 4, Reading 26 (LM 5)\tL2 FI Study Guide – Module 5: Single-Name vs. Index CDS, ISDA Conventions, Upfront Premium, Credit Curve, Basis Trades, Monetizing CDS Gains\n2.2 Key Subtopics & Core Fixed Income Formulas (Level I & Level II)\n\nFull Price, Flat Price, and Accrued Interest:\n\nPV\nFull\n=\nPV\nFlat\n+\nAI\n=\nPV\nlast coupon date\n×\n(\n1\n+\n𝑟\n)\n𝑡\n/\n𝑇\n,\nAI\n=\n𝑡\n𝑇\n×\nPMT\nPV\nFull\n=PV\nFlat\n+AI=PV\nlast coupon date\n\t​\n\n×(1+r)\nt/T\n,AI=\nT\nt\n\t​\n\n×PMT\n\nPeriodicity Conversion (Converting APR for \n𝑚\nm periods to APR for \n𝑛\nn periods):\n\n(\n1\n+\nAPR\n𝑚\n𝑚\n)\n𝑚\n=\n(\n1\n+\nAPR\n𝑛\n𝑛\n)\n𝑛\n(1+\nm\nAPR\nm\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n)\nm\n=(1+\nn\nAPR\nn\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n)\nn\n\nSpot Rates & Implied Forward Rates (\n𝑓\n𝐴\n,\n𝐵\n−\n𝐴\nf\nA,B−A\n\t​\n\n, the \n(\n𝐵\n−\n𝐴\n)\n(B−A)-period rate starting in \n𝐴\nA periods):\n\n(\n1\n+\n𝑧\n𝐵\n)\n𝐵\n=\n(\n1\n+\n𝑧\n𝐴\n)\n𝐴\n×\n(\n1\n+\n𝑓\n𝐴\n,\n𝐵\n−\n𝐴\n)\n𝐵\n−\n𝐴\n⟹\n𝑓\n𝐴\n,\n𝐵\n−\n𝐴\n=\n[\n(\n1\n+\n𝑧\n𝐵\n)\n𝐵\n(\n1\n+\n𝑧\n𝐴\n)\n𝐴\n]\n1\n𝐵\n−\n𝐴\n−\n1\n(1+z\nB\n\t​\n\n)\nB\n=(1+z\nA\n\t​\n\n)\nA\n×(1+f\nA,B−A\n\t​\n\n)\nB−A\n⟹f\nA,B−A\n\t​\n\n=[\n(1+z\nA\n\t​\n\n)\nA\n(1+z\nB\n\t​\n\n)\nB\n\t​\n\n]\nB−A\n1\n\t​\n\n−1\n\nMacaulay, Modified, Approximate, and Effective Duration & Convexity:\n\nModDur\n=\nMacDur\n1\n+\n𝑟\n,\nDuration Gap\n=\nMacDur\n−\nInvestment Horizon\nModDur=\n1+r\nMacDur\n\t​\n\n,Duration Gap=MacDur−Investment Horizon\nApproxModDur\n=\nPV\n−\n−\nPV\n+\n2\n×\nΔ\nYield\n×\nPV\n0\n,\nEffDur\n=\nPV\n−\n−\nPV\n+\n2\n×\nΔ\nCurve\n×\nPV\n0\nApproxModDur=\n2×ΔYield×PV\n0\n\t​\n\nPV\n−\n\t​\n\n−PV\n+\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n,EffDur=\n2×ΔCurve×PV\n0\n\t​\n\nPV\n−\n\t​\n\n−PV\n+\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\nApproxCon\n=\nPV\n−\n+\nPV\n+\n−\n2\n(\nPV\n0\n)\n(\nΔ\nYield\n)\n2\n×\nPV\n0\n,\nEffCon\n=\nPV\n−\n+\nPV\n+\n−\n2\n(\nPV\n0\n)\n(\nΔ\nCurve\n)\n2\n×\nPV\n0\nApproxCon=\n(ΔYield)\n2\n×PV\n0\n\t​\n\nPV\n−\n\t​\n\n+PV\n+\n\t​\n\n−2(PV\n0\n\t​\n\n)\n\t​\n\n,EffCon=\n(ΔCurve)\n2\n×PV\n0\n\t​\n\nPV\n−\n\t​\n\n+PV\n+\n\t​\n\n−2(PV\n0\n\t​\n\n)\n\t​\n\n%\nΔ\nPV\nFull\n≈\n(\n−\nAnnModDur\n×\nΔ\nYield\n)\n+\n[\n1\n2\n×\nAnnConvexity\n×\n(\nΔ\nYield\n)\n2\n]\n%ΔPV\nFull\n≈(−AnnModDur×ΔYield)+[\n2\n1\n\t​\n\n×AnnConvexity×(ΔYield)\n2\n]\n\nFloating-Rate Note (FRN) Valuation (Quoted Margin \nQM\nQM vs. Discount Margin \nDM\nDM):\n\nPV\n=\n∑\n𝑡\n=\n1\n𝑁\n(\nMRR\n+\nQM\n)\n×\nFV\n𝑚\n(\n1\n+\nMRR\n+\nDM\n𝑚\n)\n𝑡\n+\nFV\n(\n1\n+\nMRR\n+\nDM\n𝑚\n)\n𝑁\nPV=\nt=1\n∑\nN\n\t​\n\n(1+\nm\nMRR+DM\n\t​\n\n)\nt\nm\n(MRR+QM)×FV\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n+\n(1+\nm\nMRR+DM\n\t​\n\n)\nN\nFV\n\t​\n\n\nLevel II Binomial Interest Rate Tree Calibration (Adjacent Forward Rate Relation with Volatility \n𝜎\nσ):\n\n𝑟\n𝑡\n,\nupper\n=\n𝑟\n𝑡\n,\nlower\n×\n𝑒\n2\n𝜎\nΔ\n𝑡\n,\n𝑉\n0\n=\n1\n2\n[\n𝑉\n𝑈\n+\n𝐶\n1\n+\n𝑟\n0\n+\n𝑉\n𝐷\n+\n𝐶\n1\n+\n𝑟\n0\n]\nr\nt,upper\n\t​\n\n=r\nt,lower\n\t​\n\n×e\n2σ\nΔt\n\t​\n\n,V\n0\n\t​\n\n=\n2\n1\n\t​\n\n[\n1+r\n0\n\t​\n\nV\nU\n\t​\n\n+C\n\t​\n\n+\n1+r\n0\n\t​\n\nV\nD\n\t​\n\n+C\n\t​\n\n]\n\nEmbedded Options & Option-Adjusted Spread (OAS):\n\n𝑉\nCallable\n=\n𝑉\nStraight\n−\n𝑉\nCall Option\n,\n𝑉\nPutable\n=\n𝑉\nStraight\n+\n𝑉\nPut Option\nV\nCallable\n\t​\n\n=V\nStraight\n\t​\n\n−V\nCall Option\n\t​\n\n,V\nPutable\n\t​\n\n=V\nStraight\n\t​\n\n+V\nPut Option\n\t​\n\nOAS\nCallable\n=\nZ-Spread\n−\nOption Cost (in bps)\n,\nOAS\nPutable\n=\nZ-Spread\n+\nOption Benefit (in bps)\nOAS\nCallable\n\t​\n\n=Z-Spread−Option Cost (in bps),OAS\nPutable\n\t​\n\n=Z-Spread+Option Benefit (in bps)\n\nLevel II Credit Valuation Adjustment (CVA) & Risk-Neutral Credit Spread:\n\n𝑉\nRisky Bond\n=\n𝑉\nVND (Value of No Default)\n−\nCVA\n,\nCVA\n=\n∑\n𝑡\n=\n1\n𝑁\nPV\n(\nExpected Loss\n𝑡\n)\n=\n∑\n𝑡\n=\n1\n𝑁\nPOD\n𝑡\n×\nLGD\n𝑡\n×\nDF\n𝑡\nV\nRisky Bond\n\t​\n\n=V\nVND (Value of No Default)\n\t​\n\n−CVA,CVA=\nt=1\n∑\nN\n\t​\n\nPV(Expected Loss\nt\n\t​\n\n)=\nt=1\n∑\nN\n\t​\n\nPOD\nt\n\t​\n\n×LGD\nt\n\t​\n\n×DF\nt\n\t​\n\nCredit Spread\n≈\nYTM\nRisky\n−\nYTM\nBenchmark\nCredit Spread≈YTM\nRisky\n\t​\n\n−YTM\nBenchmark\n\t​\n\n\nLevel II Credit Default Swap (CDS) Pricing & Profit/Loss:\n\nUpfront Premium (\n%\n)\n≈\n(\nCredit Spread\n−\nFixed Coupon\n)\n×\nEffDur\nCDS\nUpfront Premium (%)≈(Credit Spread−Fixed Coupon)×EffDur\nCDS\n\t​\n\nCDS Price (per 100 par)\n=\n100\n−\nUpfront Premium (\n%\n)\nCDS Price (per 100 par)=100−Upfront Premium (%)\nProfit for Protection Buyer (\n%\n)\n≈\nΔ\n(\nCredit Spread in bps\n)\n×\nEffDur\nCDS\nProfit for Protection Buyer (%)≈Δ(Credit Spread in bps)×EffDur\nCDS\n\t​\n\n2.3 Graded Numerical Calculation Audit (Easy to Difficult)\n\nFundamental / Easy:\n\nCalculating Full Price, Flat Price, and Accrued Interest between coupon dates using Actual/Actual vs. 30/360 conventions.\n\nConverting money market Discount Rates (DR) on 360-day bases into Add-On Yields (AOR) on 365-day bases.\n\nComputing G-Spread (interpolated Treasury yield minus bond YTM) and I-Spread (swap rate interpolation).\n\nCalculating Money Duration (\nAnnModDur\n×\nPV\nFull\nAnnModDur×PV\nFull\n) and Price Value of a Basis Point (PVBP).\n\nIntermediate:\n\nBootstrapping Spot Rates (\n𝑧\n𝑛\nz\nn\n\t​\n\n) from par yields and computing implied forward rates (\n𝑓\n1\n,\n1\n,\n𝑓\n2\n,\n1\n,\n𝑓\n1\n,\n2\nf\n1,1\n\t​\n\n,f\n2,1\n\t​\n\n,f\n1,2\n\t​\n\n), as well as calculating the \"riding the yield curve\" horizon return when the forward curve exceeds realized spot rates.\n\nDuration + Convexity Second-Order Taylor Expansion for bond price changes, and Return Impact of a Credit Spread widening (\n%\nΔ\n𝑃\n≈\n−\nEffSpreadDur\n×\nΔ\nSpread\n+\n1\n2\nEffSpreadCon\n×\n(\nΔ\nSpread\n)\n2\n%ΔP≈−EffSpreadDur×ΔSpread+\n2\n1\n\t​\n\nEffSpreadCon×(ΔSpread)\n2\n).\n\nConvertible Bond Metrics (L1 & L2): Conversion Ratio, Conversion Value (\nStock Price\n×\nConversion Ratio\nStock Price×Conversion Ratio), Conversion Parity Price, Market Conversion Premium per Share, and Straight Value floor.\n\nComplex / Difficult (Level II Vignettes):\n\nBackward Induction on a Binomial Interest Rate Tree for Callable, Putable, and Capped/Floored Floating-Rate Bonds: Applying the call rule (\nmin\n⁡\n[\nDiscounted Node Value\n,\nCall Price\n]\nmin[Discounted Node Value,Call Price]) or put rule (\nmax\n⁡\n[\nDiscounted Node Value\n,\nPut Price\n]\nmax[Discounted Node Value,Put Price]) at each node prior to adding the coupon, and shifting every node rate up and down by \nΔ\n𝑟\nΔr while holding the OAS constant to compute Effective Duration and Effective Convexity.\n\nMulti-Period Credit Valuation Adjustment (CVA) Table Construction: Given a binomial interest rate tree (or zero-volatility benchmark curve), a hazard rate (probability of default per period), and a recovery rate:\n\nCalculate the Exposure at each date \n𝑡\nt (expected value of remaining future cash flows plus current coupon).\n\nCompute \nLGD\n𝑡\n=\nExposure\n𝑡\n×\n(\n1\n−\nRecovery Rate\n)\nLGD\nt\n\t​\n\n=Exposure\nt\n\t​\n\n×(1−Recovery Rate).\n\nCompute unconditional \nPOD\n𝑡\n=\nHazard Rate\n×\n(\n1\n−\nHazard Rate\n)\n𝑡\n−\n1\nPOD\nt\n\t​\n\n=Hazard Rate×(1−Hazard Rate)\nt−1\n (survival probability times conditional default probability).\n\nDiscount \nExpected Loss\n𝑡\n=\nLGD\n𝑡\n×\nPOD\n𝑡\nExpected Loss\nt\n\t​\n\n=LGD\nt\n\t​\n\n×POD\nt\n\t​\n\n at the risk-free spot rate \n𝑧\n𝑡\nz\nt\n\t​\n\n to sum \nCVA\nCVA, subtract \nCVA\nCVA from \nVND\nVND to find the risky bond price, and solve for the risky bond's YTM and implied credit spread.\n\nCDS Valuation, Monetization, and Cheapest-to-Deliver (CTD) Settlement: Computing the dollar upfront payment on an Investment Grade (100 bps coupon) vs. High Yield (500 bps coupon) CDS; calculating the dollar gain to a protection buyer when credit spreads widen by \n𝑋\nX bps and CDS duration drops from \n𝐷\n0\nD\n0\n\t​\n\n to \n𝐷\n1\nD\n1\n\t​\n\n; and determining the cash vs. physical settlement payout using the cheapest-to-deliver bond with the lowest market price-to-par ratio despite seniority differences.\n\n3. Equity Investments / Equity Valuation (EQ)\n3.1 Complete Learning Module & Prep Provider Cross-Reference\nLevel I Equity Investments (8 Learning Modules)\nOfficial CFAI LM #\tOfficial CFA Institute Learning Module Title (2024–2026)\tKaplan SchweserNotes Equivalent\tWiley / UWorld Equivalent & Structural Notes\nLM 1\tMarket Organization and Structure\tBook 3, Module 31 (LM 1)\tL1 Equity Study Guide – Module 1: Margin Buying, Maintenance Margin Calls, Order Types, Quote/Order Markets\nLM 2\tSecurity Market Indexes\tBook 3, Module 32 (LM 2)\tL1 Equity Study Guide – Module 2: Price, Equal, Market-Cap, Float-Adjusted & Fundamental Weighting; Index Rebalancing\nLM 3\tMarket Efficiency\tBook 3, Module 33 (LM 3)\tL1 Equity Study Guide – Module 3: Weak, Semi-Strong & Strong EMH; Market Anomalies; Behavioral Finance\nLM 4\tOverview of Equity Securities\tBook 3, Module 34 (LM 4)\tL1 Equity Study Guide – Module 4: Common vs. Preferred (Cumulative/Participating), ADRs (Sponsored/Unsponsored), ROE vs. Cost of Equity\nLM 5\tCompany Analysis: Past and Present\tBook 3, Module 35 (LM 5)\tL1 Equity Study Guide – Module 5: Revenue Drivers, Operating Leverage (DOL), Working Capital, Capital Structure\nLM 6\tIndustry and Competitive Analysis\tBook 3, Module 36 (LM 6)\tL1 Equity Study Guide – Module 6: GICS/ICB Classification, HHI Concentration Ratio, Porter's 5 Forces, PESTLE\nLM 7\tCompany Analysis: Forecasting\tBook 3, Module 37 (LM 7)\tL1 Equity Study Guide – Module 7: Top-Down vs. Bottom-Up Revenue Modeling, Cost & CapEx Projections, Scenario Analysis\nLM 8\tEquity Valuation: Concepts and Basic Tools\tBook 3, Module 38 (LM 8)\tL1 Equity Study Guide – Module 8: DDM, Gordon Growth Model, Multistage DDM, Preferred Stock, Price Multiples (P/E, P/B, EV/EBITDA), Asset-Based Valuation\nLevel II Equity Valuation (6 Learning Modules)\nOfficial CFAI LM #\tOfficial CFA Institute Learning Module Title (2024–2026)\tKaplan SchweserNotes Equivalent\tWiley / UWorld Equivalent & Structural Notes\nLM 1\tEquity Valuation: Applications and Processes\tBook 3, Reading 16 (LM 1)\tL2 Equity Study Guide – Module 1: Intrinsic vs. Going-Concern Value, Absolute vs. Relative Valuation, Sum-of-the-Parts, Conglomerate Discount\nLM 2\tDiscounted Dividend Valuation\tBook 3, Reading 17 (LM 2)\tL2 Equity Study Guide – Module 2: GGM, Implied Growth/PVGO, 2-Stage & 3-Stage DDM, H-Model, Sustainable Growth Rate (PRAT)\nLM 3\tFree Cash Flow Valuation\tBook 3, Reading 18 (LM 3)\tL2 Equity Study Guide – Module 3: FCFF & FCFE from NI/EBIT/EBITDA/CFO, Debt Ratio Forecasting, Single & Multistage FCF Models\nLM 4\tMarket-Based Valuation: Price and Enterprise Value Multiples\tBook 3, Reading 19 (LM 4)\tL2 Equity Study Guide – Module 4: Justified P/E, P/B, P/S, P/CF; PEG Ratio; Normalized EPS; Fed/Yardeni Models; EV/EBITDA; Harmonic Mean\nLM 5\tResidual Income Valuation\tBook 3, Reading 20 (LM 5)\tL2 Equity Study Guide – Module 5: EVA, MVA, Single-Stage & Multistage Residual Income (Persistence Factor \nω), Clean Surplus Violations\nLM 6\tPrivate Company Valuation\tBook 3, Reading 21 (LM 6)\tL2 Equity Study Guide – Module 6: Normalized Earnings, Expanded CAPM & Build-Up Cost of Equity, CCM, GPCM, GTM, DLOC & DLOM\n3.2 Key Subtopics & Core Equity Formulas (Level I & Level II)\n\nMargin Trading & Maintenance Margin Call Price (Level I LM 1):\n\nMargin Call Price\n=\n𝑃\n0\n×\n(\n1\n−\nInitial Margin\n1\n−\nMaintenance Margin\n)\nMargin Call Price=P\n0\n\t​\n\n×(\n1−Maintenance Margin\n1−Initial Margin\n\t​\n\n)\n\nGordon Growth Model (GGM), Present Value of Growth Opportunities (PVGO), & Justified Multiples:\n\n𝑉\n0\n=\n𝐷\n0\n(\n1\n+\n𝑔\n)\n𝑟\n−\n𝑔\n=\n𝐷\n1\n𝑟\n−\n𝑔\n,\n𝑉\n0\n=\n𝐸\n1\n𝑟\n+\nPVGO\nV\n0\n\t​\n\n=\nr−g\nD\n0\n\t​\n\n(1+g)\n\t​\n\n=\nr−g\nD\n1\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n,V\n0\n\t​\n\n=\nr\nE\n1\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n+PVGO\nJustified Leading \n𝑃\n0\n𝐸\n1\n=\n1\n−\n𝑏\n𝑟\n−\n𝑔\n,\nJustified Trailing \n𝑃\n0\n𝐸\n0\n=\n(\n1\n−\n𝑏\n)\n(\n1\n+\n𝑔\n)\n𝑟\n−\n𝑔\nJustified Leading \nE\n1\n\t​\n\nP\n0\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n=\nr−g\n1−b\n\t​\n\n,Justified Trailing \nE\n0\n\t​\n\nP\n0\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n=\nr−g\n(1−b)(1+g)\n\t​\n\nJustified \n𝑃\n0\n𝐵\n0\n=\nROE\n−\n𝑔\n𝑟\n−\n𝑔\n,\nJustified \n𝑃\n0\n𝑆\n0\n=\nNet Margin\n×\n(\n1\n−\n𝑏\n)\n(\n1\n+\n𝑔\n)\n𝑟\n−\n𝑔\nJustified \nB\n0\n\t​\n\nP\n0\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n=\nr−g\nROE−g\n\t​\n\n,Justified \nS\n0\n\t​\n\nP\n0\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n=\nr−g\nNet Margin×(1−b)(1+g)\n\t​\n\n\nSustainable Growth Rate (\n𝑔\ng) & PRAT Formula (Level I & II):\n\n𝑔\n=\n𝑏\n×\nROE\n=\n(\nNI\n−\nDiv\nNI\n)\n×\n(\nNI\nSales\n)\n×\n(\nSales\nTotal Assets\n)\n×\n(\nTotal Assets\nShareholders’ Equity\n)\ng=b×ROE=(\nNI\nNI−Div\n\t​\n\n)×(\nSales\nNI\n\t​\n\n)×(\nTotal Assets\nSales\n\t​\n\n)×(\nShareholders’ Equity\nTotal Assets\n\t​\n\n)\n\nThe H-Model (2-Stage Linearly Declining Dividend Growth Model, Level II LM 2):\n\n𝑉\n0\n=\n𝐷\n0\n(\n1\n+\n𝑔\n𝐿\n)\n+\n𝐷\n0\n×\n𝐻\n×\n(\n𝑔\n𝑆\n−\n𝑔\n𝐿\n)\n𝑟\n−\n𝑔\n𝐿\nV\n0\n\t​\n\n=\nr−g\nL\n\t​\n\nD\n0\n\t​\n\n(1+g\nL\n\t​\n\n)+D\n0\n\t​\n\n×H×(g\nS\n\t​\n\n−g\nL\n\t​\n\n)\n\t​\n\n\n(where \n𝐻\n=\n𝑁\n2\nH=\n2\nN\n\t​\n\n is the half-life of the high-growth stage of length \n𝑁\nN years, \n𝑔\n𝑆\ng\nS\n\t​\n\n is the initial short-term supernormal growth rate, and \n𝑔\n𝐿\ng\nL\n\t​\n\n is the perpetual long-term growth rate).\n\nComprehensive FCFF & FCFE Derivation Web (Level II LM 3):\n\nFCFF\n=\nNI\n+\nNCC\n+\nInt\n(\n1\n−\n𝑡\n)\n−\nFCInv\n−\nWCInv\nFCFF=NI+NCC+Int(1−t)−FCInv−WCInv\nFCFF\n=\nEBIT\n(\n1\n−\n𝑡\n)\n+\nDep\n−\nFCInv\n−\nWCInv\n=\nEBITDA\n(\n1\n−\n𝑡\n)\n+\n(\nDep\n×\n𝑡\n)\n−\nFCInv\n−\nWCInv\nFCFF=EBIT(1−t)+Dep−FCInv−WCInv=EBITDA(1−t)+(Dep×t)−FCInv−WCInv\nFCFE\n=\nFCFF\n−\nInt\n(\n1\n−\n𝑡\n)\n+\nNet Borrowing\nFCFE=FCFF−Int(1−t)+Net Borrowing\nFCFE (Target Debt Ratio DR)\n=\nNI\n−\n(\n1\n−\nDR\n)\n(\nFCInv\n−\nDep\n)\n−\n(\n1\n−\nDR\n)\n(\nWCInv\n)\nFCFE (Target Debt Ratio DR)=NI−(1−DR)(FCInv−Dep)−(1−DR)(WCInv)\nFirm Value\n=\n∑\n𝑡\n=\n1\n∞\nFCFF\n𝑡\n(\n1\n+\nWACC\n)\n𝑡\n,\nEquity Value\n=\nFirm Value\n−\nMarket Value of Debt\n=\n∑\n𝑡\n=\n1\n∞\nFCFE\n𝑡\n(\n1\n+\n𝑟\n𝑒\n)\n𝑡\nFirm Value=\nt=1\n∑\n∞\n\t​\n\n(1+WACC)\nt\nFCFF\nt\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n,Equity Value=Firm Value−Market Value of Debt=\nt=1\n∑\n∞\n\t​\n\n(1+r\ne\n\t​\n\n)\nt\nFCFE\nt\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n\nResidual Income (RI) & Persistence Factor (\n𝜔\nω) Valuation (Level II LM 5):\n\nRI\n𝑡\n=\n𝐸\n𝑡\n−\n(\n𝑟\n×\n𝐵\n𝑡\n−\n1\n)\n=\n(\nROE\n𝑡\n−\n𝑟\n)\n×\n𝐵\n𝑡\n−\n1\nRI\nt\n\t​\n\n=E\nt\n\t​\n\n−(r×B\nt−1\n\t​\n\n)=(ROE\nt\n\t​\n\n−r)×B\nt−1\n\t​\n\n𝑉\n0\n=\n𝐵\n0\n+\n(\nROE\n−\n𝑟\n)\n×\n𝐵\n0\n𝑟\n−\n𝑔\n(Constant Growth RI)\nV\n0\n\t​\n\n=B\n0\n\t​\n\n+\nr−g\n(ROE−r)×B\n0\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n(Constant Growth RI)\n𝑉\n0\n=\n𝐵\n0\n+\n∑\n𝑡\n=\n1\n𝑇\n−\n1\nRI\n𝑡\n(\n1\n+\n𝑟\n)\n𝑡\n+\nRI\n𝑇\n(\n1\n+\n𝑟\n−\n𝜔\n)\n(\n1\n+\n𝑟\n)\n𝑇\n−\n1\n(Multistage RI with Persistence Factor \n0\n≤\n𝜔\n≤\n1\n)\nV\n0\n\t​\n\n=B\n0\n\t​\n\n+\nt=1\n∑\nT−1\n\t​\n\n(1+r)\nt\nRI\nt\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n+\n(1+r−ω)(1+r)\nT−1\nRI\nT\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n(Multistage RI with Persistence Factor 0≤ω≤1)\n\nPrivate Company Valuation Discounts (DLOC & DLOM, Level II LM 6):\n\nDLOC\n=\n1\n−\n[\n1\n1\n+\nControl Premium\n]\n,\nTotal Discount\n=\n1\n−\n[\n(\n1\n−\nDLOC\n)\n×\n(\n1\n−\nDLOM\n)\n]\nDLOC=1−[\n1+Control Premium\n1\n\t​\n\n],Total Discount=1−[(1−DLOC)×(1−DLOM)]\n3.3 Graded Numerical Calculation Audit (Easy to Difficult)\n\nFundamental / Easy:\n\nComputing Price-Weighted (divisor adjustment after a stock split), Equal-Weighted, and Market-Cap Weighted index price and total returns.\n\nCalculating Enterprise Value: \nEV\n=\nMV\nCommon\n+\nMV\nPreferred\n+\nMV\nDebt\n+\nNCI\n−\nCash & Short-Term Investments\nEV=MV\nCommon\n\t​\n\n+MV\nPreferred\n\t​\n\n+MV\nDebt\n\t​\n\n+NCI−Cash & Short-Term Investments.\n\nSolving for intrinsic value using the Gordon Growth Model and computing the Implied Return (\n𝑟\n=\n𝐷\n1\n𝑃\n0\n+\n𝑔\nr=\nP\n0\n\t​\n\nD\n1\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n+g).\n\nIntermediate:\n\nTwo-Stage DDM & H-Model Valuation: Discounting explicit stage-1 dividends and a GGM terminal value (\n𝑉\n𝑛\n=\n𝐷\n𝑛\n+\n1\n𝑟\n−\n𝑔\n𝐿\nV\nn\n\t​\n\n=\nr−g\nL\n\t​\n\nD\nn+1\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n discounted back \n𝑛\nn periods at \n(\n1\n+\n𝑟\n)\n𝑛\n(1+r)\nn\n, not \n𝑛\n+\n1\nn+1 periods), or applying the H-Model formula when growth declines linearly over \n𝑁\nN years.\n\nNormalized EPS & Weighted Harmonic Mean Portfolio Multiple: Computing normalized EPS via historical average ROE (\nAverage ROE\n×\nCurrent BVPS\nAverage ROE×Current BVPS) vs. historical average EPS; calculating the benchmark index P/E using the Weighted Harmonic Mean (\n1\n∑\n𝑤\n𝑖\nP/E\n𝑖\n∑\nP/E\ni\n\t​\n\nw\ni\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n1\n\t​\n\n) to avoid upward outlier skew.\n\nComplex / Difficult (Level II Vignettes):\n\nMulti-Stage FCFF / FCFE Valuation with Non-Cash Charges & Target Debt Ratio: Extracting \nFCInv\nFCInv from \nEnding Net PP&E\n−\nBeginning Net PP&E\n+\nDepreciation\nEnding Net PP&E−Beginning Net PP&E+Depreciation (and adjusting for gains/losses on asset sales), extracting \nWCInv\nWCInv by excluding cash and short-term debt, projecting 2-stage FCFF or FCFE, discounting FCFF at WACC vs. FCFE at \n𝑟\n𝑒\nr\ne\n\t​\n\n, and reconciling why FCFF is preferred over FCFE when leverage is changing significantly.\n\nMultistage Residual Income with Persistence Factor (\n𝜔\nω) & Clean Surplus Adjustments: Building a year-by-year book value schedule (\n𝐵\n𝑡\n=\n𝐵\n𝑡\n−\n1\n+\n𝐸\n𝑡\n−\n𝐷\n𝑡\nB\nt\n\t​\n\n=B\nt−1\n\t​\n\n+E\nt\n\t​\n\n−D\nt\n\t​\n\n), calculating annual \nRI\n𝑡\n=\n𝐸\n𝑡\n−\n𝑟\n𝐵\n𝑡\n−\n1\nRI\nt\n\t​\n\n=E\nt\n\t​\n\n−rB\nt−1\n\t​\n\n, and discounting terminal residual income under three exam cases:\n\nRI continues indefinitely at current level (\n𝜔\n=\n1.0\n⟹\nPV\nTerminal\n=\nRI\n𝑇\n𝑟\n(\n1\n+\n𝑟\n)\n𝑇\n−\n1\nω=1.0⟹PV\nTerminal\n\t​\n\n=\nr(1+r)\nT−1\nRI\nT\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n).\n\nRI drops immediately to zero at \n𝑇\nT (\n𝜔\n=\n0\n⟹\nPV\nTerminal\n=\n0\nω=0⟹PV\nTerminal\n\t​\n\n=0).\n\nRI decays over time toward industry average ROE at persistence factor \n𝜔\nω (\nPV\nTerminal\n=\nRI\n𝑇\n(\n1\n+\n𝑟\n−\n𝜔\n)\n(\n1\n+\n𝑟\n)\n𝑇\n−\n1\nPV\nTerminal\n\t​\n\n=\n(1+r−ω)(1+r)\nT−1\nRI\nT\n\t​\n\n\t​\n\n), or using a terminal P/B multiple (\n𝑃\n𝑇\n=\n𝐵\n𝑇\n×\nP/B\n𝑇\n⟹\nTerminal Premium\n=\n𝑃\n𝑇\n−\n𝐵\n𝑇\nP\nT\n\t​\n\n=B\nT\n\t​\n\n×P/B\nT\n\t​\n\n⟹Terminal Premium=P\nT\n\t​\n\n−B\nT\n\t​\n\n).\n\nPrivate Company Valuation (Expanded CAPM vs. Build-Up + CCF + DLOC/DLOM): Normalizing private company earnings for owner excess compensation, personal expenses run through the business, and non-market real estate lease rates; calculating \n𝑟\n𝑒\nr\ne\n\t​\n\n via Expanded CAPM (\n𝑅\n𝑓\n+\n𝛽\n×\nERP\n+\nSP\n+\nSCRP\nR\nf\n\t​\n\n+β×ERP+SP+SCRP) vs. Build-Up Method (\n𝑅\n𝑓\n+\nERP\n+\nSP\n+\nIRP\n+\nSCRP\nR\nf\n\t​\n\n+ERP+SP+IRP+SCRP, where \n𝛽\nβ is omitted); capitalizing free cash flow via the Capitalized Cash Flow (CCF) method; and applying multiplicative DLOC and DLOM discounts to value a minority illiquid stake.\n\n4. Portfolio Management (PM)\n4.1 Complete Learning Module & Prep Provider Cross-Reference\nLevel I Portfolio Management (6 Learning Modules)\nOfficial CFAI LM #\tOfficial CFA Institute Learning Module Title (2024–2026)\tKaplan SchweserNotes Equivalent\tWiley / UWorld Equivalent & Structural Notes\nLM 1\tPortfolio Risk and Return: Part I\tBook 5, Module 64 (LM 1)\tL1 PM Study Guide – Module 1: Utility Function (\nU=E(r)−\n2\n1\n\t​\n\nAσ\n2\n), 2-Asset Portfolio Variance, Minimum-Variance & Efficient Frontiers, CAL\nLM 2\tPortfolio Risk and Return: Part II\tBook 5, Module 65 (LM 2)\tL1 PM Study Guide – Module 2: CML, Systematic vs. Nonsystematic Risk, Beta, CAPM/SML, Sharpe, Treynor, \nM\n2\n, Jensen's Alpha\nLM 3\tPortfolio Management: An Overview\tBook 5, Module 66 (LM 3)\tL1 PM Study Guide – Module 3: 3-Step PM Process, Institutional Investor Profiles (DB/DC Plans, Endowments, Banks, Insurers, SWFs), ETFs vs. Mutual Funds\nLM 4\tBasics of Portfolio Planning and Construction\tBook 5, Module 67 (LM 4)\tL1 PM Study Guide – Module 4: IPS Structure (Risk Ability vs. Willingness, Return, LLTTU Constraints), SAA vs. TAA, Core-Satellite, ESG Integration\nLM 5\tThe Behavioral Biases of Individuals\tBook 5, Module 68 (LM 5)\tL1 PM Study Guide – Module 5: Cognitive Errors (Belief Perseverance vs. Information-Processing) vs. Emotional Biases (LOS: Loss Aversion, Overconfidence, etc.)\nLM 6\tIntroduction to Risk Management\tBook 5, Module 69 (LM 6)\tL1 PM Study Guide – Module 6"